WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.506
In part one we guessed the special

2
00:00:05.582 --> 00:00:06.962
lines of the blur.

3
00:00:06.962 --> 00:00:10.257
Here is a matrix where guessing is harder

4
00:00:10.257 --> 00:00:12.020
four, one, two, three.

5
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.606
Watch what it does to five arrows.

6
00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:17.414
Most of them turn.

7
00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:20.650
Somewhere, two directions do not.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.340
How could we find them without guessing?

9
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.760
Pause and think.

10
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.829
Start from the definition.

11
00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:33.839
A v equals lambda v.

12
00:00:33.839 --> 00:00:36.614
Move everything to one side

13
00:00:36.614 --> 00:00:40.060
A minus lambda I, times v, is zero.

14
00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.949
So the matrix A minus lambda I sends

15
00:00:44.047 --> 00:00:46.505
a nonzero vector to zero.

16
00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:49.454
It crushes a whole direction

17
00:00:49.454 --> 00:00:52.010
so it flattens the plane

18
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.352
and a flat picture has area zero

19
00:00:55.352 --> 00:00:57.810
its determinant is zero.

20
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.840
That is one equation

21
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.160
and it contains only lambda.

22
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.080
Let us watch it happen.

23
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.510
Take lambda equal to zero: that is A itself

24
00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:11.922
and the square becomes a parallelogram

25
00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:13.060
of area ten.

26
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.236
Now subtract one from the diagonal.

27
00:01:16.236 --> 00:01:18.173
The area drops to four

28
00:01:18.254 --> 00:01:20.515
and the arrow v shrinks back

29
00:01:20.595 --> 00:01:22.210
to where it started.

30
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.038
Subtract one point nine.

31
00:01:25.038 --> 00:01:28.755
The parallelogram is almost a line

32
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.543
area zero point three one

33
00:01:31.543 --> 00:01:34.640
and v has nearly disappeared.

34
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.339
At lambda equal to two the plane

35
00:01:38.444 --> 00:01:41.060
falls flat onto one line

36
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.467
and v is sent to zero.

37
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:48.177
Two is an eigenvalue, and v, one, minus two

38
00:01:48.177 --> 00:01:50.270
is its eigenvector.

39
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:51.751
On that line

40
00:01:51.838 --> 00:01:54.623
A minus lambda I just multiplies

41
00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.451
by two minus lambda.

42
00:01:56.538 --> 00:01:59.322
The eigenvector is the direction

43
00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:02.020
that runs out of length first.

44
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.061
Now in symbols.

45
00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:07.568
For our matrix the determinant of A

46
00:02:07.665 --> 00:02:10.880
minus lambda I is lambda squared

47
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.802
minus seven lambda, plus ten.

48
00:02:13.802 --> 00:02:16.725
It factors: lambda minus two

49
00:02:16.725 --> 00:02:19.160
times lambda minus five.

50
00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.477
So five is the other eigenvalue.

51
00:02:23.477 --> 00:02:28.312
A minus five I is minus one, one, two

52
00:02:28.312 --> 00:02:33.890
minus two, and the vector it crushes is one

53
00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:34.510
one.

54
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.445
Each eigenvector comes from a nullspace

55
00:02:38.445 --> 00:02:40.148
as in Lecture six.

56
00:02:40.148 --> 00:02:43.913
Now apply A itself, and watch both lines.

57
00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:47.409
One, one is stretched five times, one

58
00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:49.381
minus two only twice

59
00:02:49.381 --> 00:02:51.980
and every other arrow turns.

60
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.461
Two checks come for free.

61
00:02:54.461 --> 00:02:57.275
The eigenvalues add up to the trace

62
00:02:57.275 --> 00:03:00.394
five plus two is seven, four plus three.

63
00:03:00.394 --> 00:03:03.360
They multiply to the determinant: ten.

64
00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.949
Triangular matrices are the easy case

65
00:03:06.949 --> 00:03:10.102
the eigenvalues sit on the diagonal.

66
00:03:10.102 --> 00:03:11.466
Two, one, zero

67
00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:14.023
three doubles the first axis

68
00:03:14.023 --> 00:03:16.750
and triples the arrow one, one.

69
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.685
A computer returns eigenvectors

70
00:03:19.768 --> 00:03:20.935
of length one.

71
00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:23.187
For our matrix numpy gives

72
00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.022
about seven tenths, seven tenths

73
00:03:26.022 --> 00:03:28.773
and about minus nine twentieths

74
00:03:28.773 --> 00:03:31.525
nine tenths: the same two lines

75
00:03:31.525 --> 00:03:34.860
with its own choice of length and sign.

76
00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.933
A tempting shortcut: eliminate first

77
00:03:39.028 --> 00:03:41.388
then read the eigenvalues

78
00:03:41.483 --> 00:03:43.560
off the diagonal of U

79
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.920
four and two and a half?

80
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.339
Apply U to A's two eigenvectors.

81
00:03:49.339 --> 00:03:52.798
One, one goes to five, two and a half

82
00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.040
off its line.

83
00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.500
And the other one leaves its line too.

84
00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.421
Elimination keeps the solutions

85
00:04:00.503 --> 00:04:04.069
of A x equals b, but it changes the matrix

86
00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:06.971
and so it changes the eigenvalues.

87
00:04:06.971 --> 00:04:09.210
Always work with A itself.

88
00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.925
Three small matrices with

89
00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.632
surprising answers.

90
00:04:13.718 --> 00:04:15.938
A quarter turn moves every

91
00:04:16.023 --> 00:04:17.645
arrow off its line.

92
00:04:17.645 --> 00:04:20.805
There is no real eigenvector at all.

93
00:04:20.805 --> 00:04:24.305
The equation is lambda squared plus one

94
00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:27.550
with the complex roots i and minus i.

95
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.543
A projection onto a line keeps that line

96
00:04:31.543 --> 00:04:32.964
eigenvalue one

97
00:04:32.964 --> 00:04:36.430
and kills the perpendicular direction

98
00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:37.940
eigenvalue zero.

99
00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:41.293
A shear has eigenvalue one, twice.

100
00:04:41.293 --> 00:04:44.826
But only the horizontal line stays put

101
00:04:44.826 --> 00:04:47.917
one line of eigenvectors, not two.

102
00:04:47.917 --> 00:04:51.450
Lecture nineteen shows what that costs.

103
00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.841
Last, a check on a made-up assistant answer.

104
00:04:55.841 --> 00:04:59.652
It gets five and two right, but offers one

105
00:04:59.652 --> 00:05:02.510
two as the eigenvector for five.

106
00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:04.259
Multiply it out.

107
00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:07.670
Half of one, two lands on three, four

108
00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:11.430
but five times it would be two and a half

109
00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:11.955
five.

110
00:05:12.042 --> 00:05:14.229
The trace and determinant

111
00:05:14.316 --> 00:05:15.978
checks pass anyway

112
00:05:15.978 --> 00:05:19.826
they test the eigenvalues, not the vectors.

113
00:05:19.826 --> 00:05:20.700
Run both.

114
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.611
So, to sum up

115
00:05:22.706 --> 00:05:25.938
an eigenvector is a direction that

116
00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:28.410
A minus lambda I crushes

117
00:05:28.505 --> 00:05:30.977
so the eigenvalues are the

118
00:05:31.072 --> 00:05:33.163
roots of one equation

119
00:05:33.163 --> 00:05:35.920
the determinant equals zero.

120
00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.327
Find each eigenvector from a nullspace

121
00:05:39.406 --> 00:05:41.600
check the numbers with trace

122
00:05:41.679 --> 00:05:42.933
and determinant

123
00:05:42.933 --> 00:05:45.990
and check every vector by multiplying.

124
00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.925
Next lecture puts the eigenvectors to work.

125
00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:51.641
Use them as the axes

126
00:05:51.641 --> 00:05:55.074
and the matrix becomes a list of stretches

127
00:05:55.074 --> 00:05:57.570
so its hundredth power is easy.
